; ======================================================================
;  #CAP_USB = 0 - THE DRIVERS EXIST AND THE MONITOR DOES NOT CARRY THEM.
;
;  This board has a DWC2 host controller and a LAN9514 hub-and-Ethernet
;  part behind it, and RaspberryPi3/Lib/usb_*.pbi and lan9514_*.pbi are
;  real drivers that pass their desk gates. They are the BOOT LANE's, they
;  are compiled into the serial updater, and they are NOT in this image.
;
;  That is a deliberate milestone-1 boundary rather than an oversight.
;  Bringing a USB host up on this part means DMA, and this monitor has
;  just turned its caches on for the first time; the coherency discipline
;  those drivers would need has never run here. Adding both at once would
;  mean the first failure has two candidate causes.
;
;  So the group is 0, and the `usb` family refuses in a sentence. When the
;  drivers come in, this section goes and a hw_usb.pi3 replaces it.
; ======================================================================

; What is left of the device-listing seam after the USB protocol moved
; into Anvil/Bus/usb_core.pbi: choosing WHICH device, and naming the
; driver that holds an interface. This board drives no USB host this
; monitor can reach, so a load always fails and no interface is claimed.
; A listing handed that prints the sentence about an empty bus, which is
; the truth here and not a failure to read.
